From ed45dbc15bdb3122db866362eefd0906610c2291 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: skornuta <126527569+skornuta@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 00:03:45 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 20/27] Prelude pool (VICTORY_PRELUDE_POOL=1, off): host lists
 share their preludes

Measured on the RTX 5080 (new ignored memtel card test): a command
allocator alone costs 3 KB of private memory, a closed list 228 KB, an
allocator with a list that recorded anything ~0.85 MB however little it
holds, and resetting gives none of it back. Each host list kept a prelude
of its own (some 870 in the first area): most of the ~1.95 GB the memory
minute leaves unattributed.

A prelude runs in one submission only. With the switch a submission's
preludes come from one shared pool: taken when recorded (one whose last
submission passed its fence, or a new one), tagged with the submission's
fence value when it is sent (both send paths), and idle ones past 64 are
let go. A list's own prelude is not made. Title lists keep their own
allocators: a closed list may be executed again without a reset, so its
commands must stay.

The memory CSV gets the pool's preludes alive and made.

(cherry picked from commit 4b8ff9e98b5940d8fa81e0b3a3b95c8bae1d90c0)
